NAME
       nlcache - newline cacher; creates a table of newlines

SYNOPSIS
       nlcache OPTIONS INPUT OUTPUT

       OPTIONS

       `OPTIONS`  should be a string of ASCII characters that should have their
       corresponding flag active.  Below is a comprehensive list  of  said  op-
       tions.

       Verbosity
       q  - Quiet mode   suppress information
       Q  - QUIET mode   suppress warnings
       S  - SHUTUP mode  suppress errors
       Y  - Why mode     show debug messages
       Filing
       f  - Force overwrite if output file already exists
       Output
       b  - Use binary output instead of plaintext output
       Miscellaneous
       v  - Show version information then quit
       h? - Show a short help message then quit

       Any  other  options  will be ignored.  Note that non-ASCII flags may set
       another flag that was not intended to be set.

       INPUT

       Set `INPUT` to the input stream: either a literal filename  or  `-`  for
       stdin.

       OUTPUT

       Set  `OUTPUT` to the output stream: either a literal filename or `-` for
       stdout.

DESCRIPTION
       nlcache produces a version 1 nlcache file that can be read with  nlseek.
       Version  1 nlcache files can either be in binary mode or plaintext mode.
       If a version 1 nlcache file is in binary mode, then it may  include  bi-
       nary characters.  If a version 1 nlcache file is in plaintext mode, then
       it will only contain printable ASCII characters.

       Since  the  binary  format can use more symbols than the ASCII character
       version, the binary format is approximately 50% smaller than  the  ASCII
       version, when measured in bytes.

EXIT STATUS
       If  nlcache  fails  to generate a table, it will return 1 or 2 for usage
       error and stream errors respectively.   Otherwise,  if  everything  goes
       well, nlcache will return 0.

NOTES
       Note  that  nlcache  will  reject any command that does not have exactly
       three parameters.  This means, to trigger the help message or  the  ver-
       sion message, one must include some parameters for `INPUT` and `OUTPUT`,
       though they are ignored.

       Note that nlcache does not gurantee that it will not overwrite a file if
       the  `f`  flag is not enabled.  There exists a small chance that between
       the `access()` call and the `fopen` call, a file may be created.  A file
       created in this manner will be  overwritten.   nlcache  does  this  `ac-
       cess()` check to make it less likely that one will use nlcache in a man-
       ner that is not desired.

       Note  that  the  version 1 nlcache file format can store up to one bytes
       subtracted from 4 GiB for the binary version and up to  4  GiB  for  the
       text  version.  No 4 GiB file or more can be stored, since version 1 nl-
       cache files store a 32-bit unsigned interger for  each  newline.   Since
       0xFFFFFFFF,  the  32-bit  unsigned integer limit, is used as the trailer
       for the binary format, the binary format cannot store 0xFFFFFFFF.  Forc-
       ing file past these limits will merely  silently  later  entries,  since
       they  will  only  store the 32 lesser value bits of the correct value in
       later passes.  Though, if one is using nlcache on a 4 GiB file, one  has
       bigger worries to worry about.

       nlcache  does not support writing its output to the same file as its in-
       put.  Doing so will undoubtedly cause corruption.   But  if  one  really
       does want to do it, nlcache is not guarding against this case fiercely.

